Fix an infinite busy-loop in the ostream-multiplex STREAM format

o_stream_multiplex_send_stream() jumps back to retry sending the remaining
data whenever the parent ostream accepts only part of it.  If the parent
accepts nothing at all, the loop makes no forward progress and spins
forever.  That happens on loong64, where IO_BLOCK_SIZE (8192) is smaller
than the page size (16384): once every pipe buffer is in use, the kernel
rejects a whole 8192-byte write with EAGAIN even though the last buffer
still has 8188 bytes free, so the multiplex channel leaves the parent
buffer full with data still pending.  Each iteration also allocates a write
error string on the data stack, so the stuck process grows without bound.

Return to the caller when the parent accepts nothing - it retries once the
parent has drained.  Fixes the test-lib "ostream multiplex stream
(backpressure)" hang/failure on loong64.

--- a/src/lib/ostream-multiplex.c
+++ b/src/lib/ostream-multiplex.c
@@ -247,8 +247,16 @@
 	total_sent += ret;
 	buffer_set_used_size(mstream->pending_buf, 0);
 
-	if (size > 0)
+	if (size > 0) {
+		if (ret == 0) {
+			/* The parent accepted none of the data, so there is
+			   no forward progress to be made here. Return
+			   instead of busy-looping: the caller will retry
+			   once the parent has drained. */
+			return total_sent;
+		}
 		goto again;
+	}
 	i_assert(total_sent > 0);
 	return total_sent;
 }
